'''''Musa velutina''''', the '''hairy banana'''<ref>{{PLANTS|id=MUVE|taxon=Musa velutina|accessdate=15 July 2015}}</ref> or '''pink banana''', is a [[Ploidy#Diploid|diploid]] species of [[Wild type|wild]] [[banana]].<ref>{{Cite journal|url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/4293074|jstor=4293074|title=Germination of Musa velutina Seeds: Comparison of in vivo and in vitro Systems|last1=Pancholi|first1=Naresh|last2=Wetten|first2=A.|last3=Caligari|first3=P. D. S.|journal=In Vitro Cellular & Developmental Biology. Plant|year=1995|volume=31|issue=3|pages=127–130|doi=10.1007/BF02632006|s2cid=27989888|url-access=subscription}}</ref> These plants are originally from Assam and the eastern Himalayas.<ref name="WCSP_255036">{{cite web |title=''Musa velutina''|work=[[World Checklist of Selected Plant Families]] |publisher=[[Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew]] |url=http://wcsp.science.kew.org/namedetail.do?name_id=255036 |accessdate=2018-07-05}}</ref>

The fruits are {{convert|3|in|cm|0|abbr=on}} long, pink, and fuzzy. They are borne on erect flower stalks with a pink [[inflorescence]]. ''Musa velutina'' flowers at a young age, doing so within a year. The fruits peel back when ripe.

It is often grown as an ornamental plant, but has soft, sweet flesh that can be eaten. The seeds are quite tough and can chip a tooth. To sow, first soak the seeds in warm water for 24 hours. They should be planted in fine [[compost]] and kept at a constant temperature of {{convert|20|-|24|C|F}} with full natural light. They take up to around 6 months to germinate.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://homeguides.sfgate.com/germinate-pink-banana-plant-50815.html|title = How to Germinate a Pink Banana Plant}}</ref>

Plants may be placed outside during warmer months but should be taken into a conservatory or greenhouse or given other protection in winter. ''Musa velutina'' has received the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/11240/Musa-velutina/Details | title = ''Musa veltina'' | website = www.rhs.org | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 3 January 2021}}</ref>

